WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.690
KEVIN NELSON: Cloud Firestore
is a serverless NoSQL document

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.540
database that simplifies
storing, syncing,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.550
and querying data for
your mobile and web

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.276
apps at global scale.

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.150
You may have heard of
that Cloud Firestore is

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.220
the next generation
of Cloud Datastore,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.079
but what does that mean?

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.400
What makes it different?

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.610
And what should you do if you're
an existing Datastore customer?

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:21.750
I'm Kevin Nelson.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.250
Find out answers to all
those questions and more

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:25.780
in this video.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.340
Let's get the question I always
get asked first out of the way.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.640
I want to start off by assuring
Datastore users that all

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.340
your existing Datastore
applications will continue

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.060
to work under Cloud Firestore
running in Datastore mode.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.310
You won't have to
make any code changes

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.550
and you'll experience zero
downtime when your service is

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.670
automatically upgraded after
Cloud Firestore becomes

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.180
generally available.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.540
So with that out of the
way, let's do a quick review

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.640
of what Datastore
is and then I'll

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.680
get into the new
capabilities being

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.140
introduced in Cloud Firestore.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.250
Google Cloud Datastore is a
fully managed, NoSQL document

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.970
database, built for automatic
scaling, high performance,

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.540
and ease of application
development.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.620
It was introduced with
Google App Engine--

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.560
Google's original serverless
development platform

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.900
that today, handles
the toughest workloads

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.150
from some of the world's
most popular apps.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.270
Cloud Firestore
builds on the features

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.820
and massive scalability
of Datastore

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.490
while adding several
important new features.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.800
The first thing to understand
is that Cloud Firestore

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.110
runs in two mutually
exclusive modes--

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.600
Datastore mode and Native mode.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.730
In Datastore mode,
Cloud Firestore

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.460
runs all your existing
Datastore applications

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.190
and eliminates some of
Datastore's restrictions.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:46.700
Cloud Firestore is built on
an entirely new back end that

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.160
brings the best of Google
Cloud's powerful cloud

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:52.860
infrastructure to existing
Datastore applications.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.950
This includes
strong consistency,

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.590
automatic multi-region
data replication,

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:01.160
atomic batch operations,
and transaction support.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.980
Perhaps even better,
Cloud Firestore completely

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.470
eliminates the entity
group transaction limits

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.389
that were present in Datastore.

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:10.850
Cloud Firestore has
no entity groups

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.460
and therefore, no
entity group limits.

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.790
You get all of these new
capabilities and compatibility

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.060
with Datastore when running
Cloud Firestore in Datastore

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.260
mode.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.230
Cloud Firestore Native
mode has all the advantages

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.390
of Datastore mode,
but also contains

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.310
a number of new
features that make

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.060
it easier to power native,
mobile, and web apps, including

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.300
robust multi-device
synchronization, full offline

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.480
support, and a comprehensive
set of client libraries,

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.010
which support an
integrated storage

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.310
solution across Google
App Engine, Kubernetes,

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.270
and mobile devices.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:49.179
You can learn more about this
using the link to my left.

00:02:49.179 --> 00:02:50.720
It's important to
understand, though,

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.440
that Cloud Firestore Native mode
isn't compatible with existing

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.030
Datastore applications.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.850
You'll have to set up a new
project with a new Cloud

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.180
Firestore database if you
want to use Native mode.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.140
In either mode, Cloud Firestore
is a truly serverless database

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.210
service that eliminates
the need to think

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.520
about spinning up
virtual machines, data

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.500
replication, or scaling.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.810
Should your app hit the
first page of Techmeme

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:18.650
and suddenly grow from
hundreds of QPS to millions,

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.450
Cloud Firestore
will automatically

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.090
scale to handle that
surge in traffic.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.770
And congratulations.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:27.680
The data model in Cloud
Firestore Native mode

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.870
is similar to the
Datastore data model,

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.330
but there are a few differences.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.250
Data and Cloud
Firestore is organized

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.650
into documents and
collections, which

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:40.250
are similar to entities
and kinds in Datastore.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.980
At the highest level, a
Cloud Firestore database

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.830
can have any number of
collections, which contain

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:47.720
any number of documents.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.810
A document contains a
group of key value pairs

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:54.140
called fields in Firestore,
similar to properties

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.610
in Datastore.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.890
Documents can also
contain sub-collections

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.510
of additional documents.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:03.110
Notice the alternating pattern
of collections and documents.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.240
Your collections and
documents must always

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:06.650
follow this pattern.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:09.230
You cannot reference a
collection in a collection

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:12.200
or a document within a document.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.180
Managing indexes
in Cloud Firestore

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:15.860
is also a little
different than how

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:17.600
they're managed in Datastore.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.180
Like Datastore, Cloud
Firestore requires

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.470
an index for every query.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.860
Firestore automatically
creates an index

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.040
for every document field.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.330
Compound queries
require that you first

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:32.150
create a composite index
that maps to the range

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.650
clause of your query.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:35.210
These are easy to
manage directly

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:36.980
from the Firestore console.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.680
You'll even be prompted to
create the composite index

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.540
the first time you
attempt a query that

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.660
doesn't have an existing index.

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:47.600
When you create a new
Cloud Firestore database,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.490
you'll select which
mode you want--

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:52.970
either Datastore mode or
Firestore Native mode.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:54.890
Consider this choice
carefully because you

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:57.560
won't be able to change the
mode once the database has

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.440
been created.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.070
As I mentioned earlier, to
take full advantage of all

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.530
of the new capabilities of Cloud
Firestore like offline support

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:08.590
and device synchronization,
existing Datastore users

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.620
will need to set up a new
project with a new database

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.950
in Firestore Native mode.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.870
This was a very
basic introduction

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.730
to the Cloud
Firestore data model.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.490
You can learn more
in my colleague Todd

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:22.770
Kerpelman's video series,
Get to Know Cloud Firestore,

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.460
on the Google Cloud
YouTube channel and online

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:29.010
at cloud.google.com/firestore.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.620
There you can learn
all about Cloud

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:33.330
Firestore's [? scheme-less ?]
data model and advanced query

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:34.545
capabilities.

00:05:34.545 --> 00:05:35.820
OK.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.800
So now you're probably
asking yourself,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.720
Firestore sounds
great, but what does

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.350
it really mean for my existing
application using Datastore?

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.510
Great question.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:49.500
During beta, Datastore
customers-- or really, anyone--

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.470
can try out Firestore by
creating a Firestore database

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:53.950
in a new project.

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.130
Select Firestore under
the Storage options

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.960
in the GCP console.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.780
If you want to move existing
data into Firestore,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.057
you can do so using the Managed
Export and Import service.

00:06:04.057 --> 00:06:05.640
Once Cloud Firestore
has been launched

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.430
for general availability,
all Datastore projects

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:10.860
will be automatically
migrated to Firestore

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.900
running in Datastore mode.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.910
The upgrade will be done
automatically for you

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:18.750
with no downtime and no
changes to your application.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:22.530
Absolutely no action is
required on your part.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.150
Migrating all
Datastore customers

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.380
will likely take a few months.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.170
You'll be notified
when your migration is

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:29.520
scheduled to begin.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.220
If that time isn't inconvenient
or you have a critical time

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.160
window where you don't want
your application to possibly be

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:38.970
impacted by the automatic
migration-- say Black Friday--

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:42.220
you'll be able to indicate
preferences via a Google form.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:45.360
But again, you shouldn't
notice any differences

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.000
at all during or
after migration.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.570
So now you may be wondering
whether your next project

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.630
should be built using Cloud
Firestore in Datastore mode

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:56.190
or Native mode.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.500
In general, we recommend
building new applications

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.500
in Native mode so your app
can take advantage of all

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.220
Firestore's capabilities.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.610
But keep in mind that
during the beta period,

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.220
Cloud Firestore
won't have an SLA.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:10.094
And Cloud Firestore
in Native mode

00:07:10.094 --> 00:07:11.760
may have some
restrictions on the number

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:14.640
of concurrent connections and
the maximum number of writes

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:15.840
per second.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:18.720
Those caps may rise as
we get closer to GA.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.570
Refer to the documentation
for the latest details.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.190
So Cloud Firestore
in Native mode

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.010
lets you take advantage of
Firestore's full capabilities,

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:29.130
but may not be appropriate
for large, mission critical,

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:33.930
enterprise applications until
Cloud Firestore goes GA.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.750
So to recap, Cloud Firestore
is the next generation

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.010
of Datastore.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:39.810
All existing Datastore
databases will

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.940
be migrated to Cloud
Firestore running

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.010
in Datastore mode
beginning shortly

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:45.750
after general availability.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.520
That migration will be
automatic and seamless.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:52.320
Firestore in Native mode adds
powerful new capabilities

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.090
for web and mobile developers,
like comprehensive client

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:58.200
libraries, offline support,
and automatic device

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.000
synchronization.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.760
I hope this video has given
you a better idea about some

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.575
of the new capabilities
of Firestore

00:08:04.575 --> 00:08:06.450
and what it means for
your existing Datastore

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:07.590
applications.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.050
You can find lots more
information about Firestore

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:13.560
on the Google Cloud YouTube
channel and in the online docs.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:15.566
You'll find those
links right next to me.

00:08:15.566 --> 00:08:16.440
Thanks for listening.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

